---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';
import { TEA_INVESTMENT_THRESHOLD } from '@/lib/eb5-investment';

const title = 'EB-5 Visa for Indian Investors: A 2026 Guide | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for Indian investors covers investment requirements, India chargeability basics, I-526E filing, and project selection for Indian nationals evaluating the program in 2026.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-indian-investors`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 Visa for Indian Investors: A 2026 Guide for Indian Nationals';
const subhead =
  'What Indian nationals need to know about investment requirements, visa category timing, and the filing process before pursuing an EB-5 green card.';

const teaFormatted = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'USD',
  maximumFractionDigits: 0,
}).format(TEA_INVESTMENT_THRESHOLD);

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Guide for Indian Professionals — 2026',
    url: '/research/eb5-guide-indian-professionals-2026',
    description: 'Program structure and decision framing for Indian professionals evaluating EB-5.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Best Immigration Backup Plan for Indian Tech Workers',
    url: '/research/best-immigration-backup-plan-indian-tech-workers-2026',
    description: 'Why many Indian H-1B holders treat EB-5 as a parallel planning track.',
  },
];
---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone Who Understands EB-5 for Indian Investors"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for Indian Investors"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for Indian investors',
    'EB-5 India 2026',
    'Indian nationals EB-5',
    'EB-5 visa India',
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="extraCompliance">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed border border-border rounded-lg bg-secondary/40 p-4 m-0">
      EB-5 investment involves financial risk, including potential loss of principal, and does not guarantee a green
      card, visa availability, or any specific processing time. Consult a qualified immigration attorney and a
      FINRA-registered financial adviser for guidance specific to your situation.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <p>
    The EB-5 Immigrant Investor Program gives eligible foreign nationals a path to a U.S. green card through a
    qualifying investment. Indian nationals — including investors abroad and professionals already in the U.S. on
    nonimmigrant status — evaluate the program for different reasons than many other country groups, largely because
    employment-based categories chargeable to India have historically faced significant demand. This guide covers what
    Indian investors need to evaluate before filing.
  </p>

  <h2>Investment requirements</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5 requires a qualifying investment in a new commercial enterprise, structured to create the required number of
    U.S. jobs. Most investors use the reduced Targeted Employment Area (TEA) investment threshold of {teaFormatted}
    rather than the standard amount — see our full
    <a href="/research/eb5-economic-calculus-investment-cost">EB-5 Investment Cost breakdown</a> for current figures.
    These thresholds cover the capital investment amount only; they do not include USCIS filing fees, regional center
    administrative fees, legal fees, or other costs.
  </p>

  <h2>Visa category and timing — what "country of birth" means for Indian investors</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5 visa numbers are allocated by country of birth (chargeability), not current citizenship or residence. India
    has historically experienced significant EB-2 and EB-3 employment-based backlogs under per-country limits, which is
    part of why some Indian professionals evaluate EB-5's different visa allocation mechanics — including reserved
    categories such as rural and infrastructure set-asides. EB-5 still has its own category rules and demand patterns;
    it is not a guaranteed shortcut. Visa availability changes over time. Investors should review current
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    > data and discuss their specific timeline with an immigration attorney rather than relying on general estimates. For
    program structure context, see the
    <a href="/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide">complete EB-5 guide</a> and our
    <a href="/research/eb5-guide-indian-professionals-2026">EB-5 guide for Indian professionals</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The filing process, in brief</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Select a project and complete source-of-funds documentation</li>
    <li>
      File Form I-526E (with concurrent adjustment of status filing for investors already in the U.S. on a valid
      nonimmigrant status, or consular processing for investors abroad)
    </li>
    <li>Receive work authorization and travel document during the concurrent filing period, where applicable</li>
    <li>Await visa number availability and I-526E adjudication</li>
    <li>File Form I-829 to remove conditions on permanent residence</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For the full step-by-step guide, see the
    <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing a project</h2>
  <p>
    Project selection is one of the most consequential decisions in the EB-5 process. Regional centers with USCIS
    I-956F approval have had their compliance and job-creation methodology reviewed by USCIS, which is one factor —
    not the only factor — in evaluating a project. See our
    <a href="/research/19-questions-rural-eb5-project-due-diligence">due diligence guide</a> and
    <a href="/research/choosing-rural-eb5-regional-center-guide">rural EB-5 regional center guide</a> for the fuller list
    of questions to ask before committing capital.
  </p>

  <h2>For students and H-1B professionals</h2>
  <div class="not-prose grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
    <a
      href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-students"
      class="block rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-150"
    >
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">EB-5 for Indian International Students</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        If you're currently on F-1 status and evaluating EB-5 as an alternative to the H-1B path, start here.
      </p>
    </a>
    <a
      href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-h1b"
      class="block rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6 hover:border-primary hover:shadow-md transition-all duration-150"
    >
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">EB-5 for Indian H-1B Professionals</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        If you're on H-1B and weighing EB-5 against India EB-2/EB-3 backlog dynamics, start here.
      </p>
    </a>
  </div>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
